{"step_index":866,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-13T15:28:48Z","input_tokens":2868,"cache_read_tokens":211825,"output_tokens":888,"thinking":"The project's `package.json` lacks an \"engines\" field, potentially leading to Nixpacks using an incompatible Node.js version during the build process, specifically an older version (like 18 or 20), whereas Next.js 16 requires Node.js 20 or higher. Adding an \"engines\" field specifying a minimum Node.js version will resolve this issue. Additionally, configuring the build environment to explicitly set `NIXPACKS_NODE_VERSION` will ensure the correct Node.js version.\n\n\nA `postinstall` script, specifically Prisma's generation step triggered by `npm ci` within a Docker environment, is a potential failure point. The Prisma configuration necessitates specifying `binaryTargets` when developing on Windows and deploying to Linux; this configuration is present in the `schema.prisma`. Platform-specific lockfiles, particularly from Windows, may cause Prisma engine download failures in Linux containers without these targets. The deployment tool's default install command, `npm ci`, could fail due to lockfile inconsistencies or peer dependency conflicts, providing alternative install commands as solutions.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:/codekids/prisma/schema.prisma\"","EndLine":"15","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Viewing prisma schema generator\"","toolSummary":"\"Prisma generator check\""}}]}
